            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You've seen Hindi's sir and hāth trace all the way back to Sanskrit. Tamil's words for the same two body parts tell a simpler story: both are native."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: The words",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "தலை (talai) = \"head\" — native Dravidian."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "கை (kai) = \"hand\" — native Dravidian."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Neither is borrowed from Sanskrit, unlike Hindi's sir (< Sanskrit śiras) or hāth (< Sanskrit hasta). Tamil simply kept its own words for two of the most basic parts of the body."
            }
          ]
        },
